WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.633
While the secret garden was coming alive and two children were coming alive with it, Mr. Craven was wandering through Europe.

2
00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:15.896
He had traveled through the beautiful mountains of Switzerland.

3
00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:19.522
He had seen the magnificent Norwegian fjords.

4
00:00:19.523 --> 00:00:26.282
But his heart was still broken, and he could not shake the dark thoughts and nightmares from his mind.

5
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:31.153
One day he was hiking in Austria when he decided to rest by a stream.

6
00:00:31.154 --> 00:00:37.846
Mr. Craven did not know how long he sat there on the carpet of green moss, looking at the birds and the flowers.

7
00:00:37.847 --> 00:00:41.456
The sunlight seemed to wash over him.

8
00:00:41.457 --> 00:00:44.701
"What's happening?" he whispered.

9
00:00:44.702 --> 00:00:48.530
"I feel . . . Why, it's almost as if I were awakening!"

10
00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:57.743
Months later he would find out that this was the very day when Colin stood in the garden and said, "I'm going to live forever and ever!"

11
00:00:57.744 --> 00:01:03.781
As summer changed into autumn, Mr. Craven moved on to Lake Como in Italy.

12
00:01:03.782 --> 00:01:08.315
He was starting to sleep better, and his dreams no longer terrified him.

13
00:01:08.316 --> 00:01:12.908
He began to think of Misselthwaite and wonder if he should go home.

14
00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:17.345
But every time he thought of Colin, he was reminded of his wife.

15
00:01:17.346 --> 00:01:20.097
He couldn't go home yet.

16
00:01:20.098 --> 00:01:23.251
One night he sat outside by the lake.

17
00:01:23.252 --> 00:01:28.757
The moonlight turned everything to silver. Mr. Craven fell asleep.

18
00:01:28.758 --> 00:01:35.664
In his dreams he heard a voice calling him. "Archie! Archie!"

19
00:01:35.665 --> 00:01:41.817
It was a sweet and lovely voice that he instantly recognized as his wife's.

20
00:01:41.818 --> 00:01:44.619
"Where are you?"

21
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.874
"In the garden."

22
00:01:46.875 --> 00:01:50.984
The dream ended there, but he did not wake up until morning.

23
00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:56.443
A servant appeared by his side and handed him a letter from Dickon's mother.

24
00:01:56.444 --> 00:02:03.788
Dear Sir: Please come home. I think you will be glad if you do.

25
00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:10.691
If you will excuse me, sir, I think your lady would ask you to come if she was here.

26
00:02:10.692 --> 00:02:14.664
Sincerely, Susan Sowerby

27
00:02:14.665 --> 00:02:17.737
Mr. Craven remembered his dream.

28
00:02:17.738 --> 00:02:25.058
"I must go home to Misselthwaite at once," he said to the servant.

29
00:02:25.059 --> 00:02:32.305
When Mr. Craven finally arrived at the manor, he immediately sent for Mrs. Medlock.

30
00:02:32.306 --> 00:02:34.849
"How is Colin?"

31
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:38.140
Mrs. Medlock didn't know how to respond.

32
00:02:38.141 --> 00:02:46.355
"To tell the truth, sir," she said nervously, "he might be better and he might be worse.

33
00:02:46.356 --> 00:02:53.317
No one can understand why he eats or doesn't eat. He likes Mary and Dickon.

34
00:02:53.318 --> 00:02:59.917
Every day he goes outside with them, but no one knows what they do or where they

35
00:02:59.918 --> 00:03:02.100
go." "Where is Colin

36
00:03:02.101 --> 00:03:05.814
now?" "In one of the gardens, I

37
00:03:05.815 --> 00:03:10.799
suppose." "In the garden," Mr. Craven repeated as he stood

38
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:18.165
up. He felt as if he were being pulled toward the garden that he had locked up ten years

39
00:03:18.166 --> 00:03:22.167
ago. He went outside and walked past the old

40
00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:26.512
fountain. Flowers surrounded it now, and it was filled with

41
00:03:26.513 --> 00:03:30.671
water. He turned down the long path outside the walled

42
00:03:30.672 --> 00:03:34.881
gardens. He knew where the door was, even though it was hidden by

43
00:03:34.882 --> 00:03:37.443
ivy. But could he find the

44
00:03:37.444 --> 00:03:39.792
key? He stopped and

45
00:03:39.793 --> 00:03:46.968
listened. No one had passed through that door in ten years, yet it sounded like children were laughing in the

46
00:03:46.969 --> 00:03:53.137
garden. Was this another dream? At that moment the door to the garden flew

47
00:03:53.138 --> 00:03:57.576
open. A boy ran out and crashed right into Mr.

48
00:03:57.577 --> 00:04:00.228
Craven. Mr. Craven stared at the

49
00:04:00.229 --> 00:04:06.097
boy. His eyes were so dark, so like her eyes . . . But it couldn't

50
00:04:06.098 --> 00:04:09.576
be. "Father, I'm Colin," said the

51
00:04:09.577 --> 00:04:14.694
boy. "I know you can't believe it. I can scarcely believe it

52
00:04:14.695 --> 00:04:20.184
myself." "In the garden," said Mr. Craven, unable to say anything

53
00:04:20.185 --> 00:04:23.543
else. "Yes. It was the garden that made me

54
00:04:23.544 --> 00:04:27.984
well. And Mary. And Dickon and his creatures. And

55
00:04:27.985 --> 00:04:32.814
magic." Colin was breathless from running, and his words came tumbling

56
00:04:32.815 --> 00:04:36.617
out. "I'm really well. I just beat Mary in a

57
00:04:36.618 --> 00:04:41.187
race. Father, I'm going to live forever and ever. Aren't you

58
00:04:41.188 --> 00:04:44.759
glad?" Mr. Craven did not know what to say for a

59
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.856
moment. He put his hands on Colin's shoulders. "You must tell me all about

60
00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:54.620
it." And so Colin led him into the secret

61
00:04:54.621 --> 00:05:02.886
garden. Mr. Craven looked around, just as Colin had done his first time there, marveling at the roses and other

62
00:05:02.887 --> 00:05:06.037
